# Convex local-deployment state. Ignored by `.convex/.gitignore`, but prettier
# only reads the ROOT `.gitignore` — without this entry a local `format:check`
# goes red on a file CI (a fresh checkout) never even sees.
.convex

# Vendored agent skills, installed by `npx convex ai-files install` and
# committed verbatim. Third-party output — reformatting it would only fight the
# next install. Their drift is what turned the `quality` job red for six weeks.
.agents
skills-lock.json

# Worklist importer staging output (ADR 0041) — regenerable, gitignored
data/worklists/*.out

# Per-machine Claude Code permissions. Ignored via the user's GLOBAL git ignore
# (`~/.config/git/ignore`), which prettier cannot see at all — it reads only the
# repo's root `.gitignore`. CI checks out a tree where this file doesn't exist,
# so the drift is invisible there and red on every local `check:pr`.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Oracle compiler lockfile (ADR 0105). Generated by `bun run oracle:compile`
# with a deterministic serializer that puts ONE ROW PER LINE — 35,000 rows
# formatted by prettier would be 400,000 lines and every regeneration an
# unreadable diff, which defeats the reason the file is committed at all
# (a card moving ready -> unparsed must be one changed line in review).
data/oracle-compiled.json

# Oracle retirement ledger (issue #3049, ADR 0114 §1). Same rationale as the
# entry above: written by `bun run oracle:retire` with a deterministic
# serializer that puts ONE RETIREMENT PER LINE, so a retirement is a single
# added line in review. Prettier would explode each entry across five lines and
# collapse the empty-array form, and the script's own output would then never
# reproduce the committed bytes.
data/oracle-retirements.json

# The merged catalogue artifact (issue #3052, ADR 0114 §2) — minified and
# content-addressed by `scripts/catalogue-artifact.ts`. Same rationale as the
# lockfile above: prettier would rewrite the committed
# bytes on every `bun run format`, so `catalogue:pack` would no longer
# reproduce what is on disk and the file name would stop matching its content.
data/catalogue/

# The CR citation ledger (issue #3674, ADR 0133). Written by `bun run cr:ledger`
# with a deterministic serializer that puts ONE CITATION PER LINE (~30k of
# them), so a confirmation is a single changed line in review and two branches
# confirming different citations merge without a conflict. Prettier would
# explode each entry across five lines and the command's own output would then
# never reproduce the committed bytes — same rationale as the ledgers above.
data/cr/citations-ledger.json


# The Bot Reach findings artifact (issue #4175, ADR 0141 §4). Same rationale as
# the ledgers above: `bun run bot:reach` writes it with a deterministic
# serializer that puts ONE FINDING PER LINE, so a card changing verdict is a
# single changed line in review and the file IS the sweep's verdict cache.
# Prettier explodes each row across ten lines (measured: 816 -> 6,407), and the
# command's own output would then never reproduce the committed bytes.
data/bot-reach-findings.json
